2 TITL Lemuel Glymph
1 _OID 423d7d0f-e248-446b-b860-fb8360117909
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;son of John and Elizabeth was born Jan 7, 1795. He lost his father when he was 12 years old and began to assist his mother in the affairs of his family. The settlement of his father&amp;#39;s will indicated tha
2 CONC t Lemuel and his siblings were to be privately tutored. His father&amp;#39;s estate was finalized March 15, 1824 and by that time Lemuel had his own plantation. He married Sarah Kinard and their first child was born in 1826. Their tenth child was b
2 CONC orn in 1848 just two years before his death.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Lemuel was a very successful planter, who at the time of his death was worth more than $50,000 and he owned 58 slaves. His death occured 15 days before South Carolina seceded from the u
2 CONC nion. On Jan. 30, 1836, Lemuel contracted with David Ergle to construct a school house. He provided some education for the slave children. Lemuel was reputed to be a good and kind master to his slaves, while strong willed Sarah was not as easy wit
2 CONC h them. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Lemuel died intestate. His estate was administered during the war.&amp;nbsp; His two elder sons, Josephus (Flavious Joseph) and John served as adminstrators. His two brother-in-laws, John M. and H.H. Kinard executed persn
2 CONC al bonds in the amount of $100,000 to secure their faithful performance.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Lemuel is buried in the Eargle-Sligh Cemetery, Glymphville, South Carolina.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Notes for Lemuel Glymph from&amp;n
2 CONC bsp; Cuanrd-Kelly-Lovinggood-Lynn family.&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
